        {
          "id": "art-avant-gardes",
          "name": "Early 20th-Century Avant-Gardes",
          "state": "not-started",
          "completedAt": null,
          "prerequisites": [
            "art-impressionism"
          ],
          "summary": "Between roughly 1905 and 1930, a rapid succession of European avant-gardes broke with inherited ideas about representation, each advancing a distinct argument: that color could carry emotion directly, that painting should register psychological and social anguish, that a single fixed viewpoint falsified how objects exist in space and time, and that art should embrace the speed and violence of the machine. The movements build from expressive reinventions of color and form through Cubism's fracturing of perspective toward increasingly radical challenges to the very purpose of art, culminating in Dada's rejection of aesthetic value and Surrealism's turn to dreams and the unconscious as the true subject of modern work.",
