          "id": "art-pop-conceptual",
          "name": "Pop Art, Minimalism, and Conceptual Art",
          "state": "not-started",
          "completedAt": null,
          "prerequisites": [
            "art-mid-century"
          ],
          "summary": "Beginning with Pop Art's embrace of mass culture and consumer imagery as legitimate subject matter, the sequence moves to Minimalism's insistence that an artwork be nothing more than its literal physical presence, then to Conceptual Art's radical claim that the idea itself, rather than any crafted object, constitutes the work. A closing turn to photography examines how a reproducible, mechanical medium came to be argued for as fine art, extending the unit's central question about what qualifies as art and where its meaning resides.",
